From: Sergio Martin
Subject: [PATCH] chimera: a clipped noperspective value takes each end's share from the W values

When the clipper makes a new vertex, the values a shader asked to be
interpolated without perspective must be the straight line across the
screen between the edge's two ends. Upstream worked out where the new
vertex lies on that line by dividing both ends by W and taking a ratio
along one screen axis, then blended the ends' values with it. For an end
just behind the eye (W a little below 0) its position on the screen, and
the values a shader computes from it, are enormous, and the blend
cancelled to noise. Supermodel's New3D draws four-cornered polygons with
such values, and floors and roads reaching behind the camera came out
smeared (Chimera issues 479 and 493). Each end's share now comes straight
from the W values: at a fraction t along the edge from out to in, in's
share is t * W(in) / W(new) and out's (1 - t) * W(out) / W(new).
---
diff -ru a/src/gallium/auxiliary/draw/draw_pipe_clip.c b/src/gallium/auxiliary/draw/draw_pipe_clip.c
--- a/src/gallium/auxiliary/draw/draw_pipe_clip.c
+++ b/src/gallium/auxiliary/draw/draw_pipe_clip.c
@@ -180,31 +180,31 @@
       interp_attr(dst->data[attr], t, in->data[attr], out->data[attr]);
    }
 
-   /**
-    * Compute the t in screen-space instead of 3d space to use
-    * for noperspective interpolation.
-    *
-    * The points can be aligned with the X axis, so in that case try
-    * the Y.  When both points are at the same screen position, we can
-    * pick whatever value (the interpolated point won't be in front
-    * anyway), so just use the 3d t.
+   /* Chimera: a value the shader asked to be interpolated without
+    * perspective is a straight line across the screen, so the new vertex
+    * takes each end's share of the screen-space distance. Upstream worked
+    * that share out by dividing both ends by W and taking a ratio along one
+    * axis; for an end just behind the eye (W a little below 0) its position
+    * on the screen is enormous, and the share and the values the shader gave
+    * that end (which are enormous too) cancelled to noise: Supermodel's
+    * floors and roads reaching behind the camera came out smeared (Chimera
+    * issues 479, 493). Each share comes straight from the W values instead:
+    * at a fraction t along the edge from out to in, in's share is
+    * t * W(in) / W(new) and out's (1 - t) * W(out) / W(new), and neither is
+    * one minus a number close to one.
     */
    if (clip->num_linear_attribs) {
-      float t_nopersp = t;
-      /* find either in.x != out.x or in.y != out.y */
-      for (int k = 0; k < 2; k++) {
-         if (in->clip_pos[k] != out->clip_pos[k]) {
-            /* do divide by W, then compute linear interpolation factor */
-            float in_coord = in->clip_pos[k] / in->clip_pos[3];
-            float out_coord = out->clip_pos[k] / out->clip_pos[3];
-            float dst_coord = dst->clip_pos[k] / dst->clip_pos[3];
-            t_nopersp = (dst_coord - out_coord) / (in_coord - out_coord);
-            break;
-         }
+      const float w_new = dst->clip_pos[3];
+      float share_in = t * in->clip_pos[3] / w_new;
+      float share_out = (1.0f - t) * out->clip_pos[3] / w_new;
+      if (!isfinite(share_in) || !isfinite(share_out)) {
+         share_in = t;
+         share_out = 1.0f - t;
       }
       for (unsigned j = 0; j < clip->num_linear_attribs; j++) {
          const unsigned attr = clip->linear_attribs[j];
-         interp_attr(dst->data[attr], t_nopersp, in->data[attr], out->data[attr]);
+         for (unsigned k = 0; k < 4; k++)
+            dst->data[attr][k] = share_out * out->data[attr][k] + share_in * in->data[attr][k];
       }
    }
 }
